The following statements are run, in order, on an empty PostgreSQL database. If a statement fails with an error, it has no effect and the remaining statements still run.

CREATE TABLE t (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, grp INTEGER, v INTEGER);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 2, 6);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (2, 1, 6);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (3, 3, 3);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (4, 3, 9);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (5, 2, 1);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (6, 1, 8);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (7, 1, 1);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (8, 2, 3);

Then this query is run:

SELECT grp, SUM(v) FROM t GROUP BY grp ORDER BY grp;

What does the query return?

End your reply with a final line of the form 'Answer: [(1, 5), (2, 7)]' that lists the returned (grp, value) rows in order, writing NULL for a null value.

